WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.610
The last episode.

2
00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:07.000
We close the developer track, and the whole series, by looking at the project's own planning documents — where it started, what actually shipped, and what's genuinely still open.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:07.367
docs/PLAN.

4
00:00:07.367 --> 00:00:13.541
md is the original master plan — phase 1, a very simple chat app over any endpoint, deliberately excluding local hosting, MCP, attachments, RAG, projects, multi-window.

5
00:00:13.541 --> 00:00:15.304
Phase 2 was LM Studio-style local model hosting.

6
00:00:15.304 --> 00:00:17.362
Phase 3 was server mode and Claude Desktop-style extras.

7
00:00:17.362 --> 00:00:21.000
Reading it now, next to the app you've spent seventy-three episodes in, is the point of this slide.

8
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:21.660
Now, versus then.

9
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:32.254
Everything explicitly deferred in phase 1 — projects, knowledge RAG, MCP — shipped anyway, along with a great deal the original plan never mentioned at all: voice and 3D avatars, the device mesh, swarms, the entire browser build with its in-tab Linux VM, evals, benchmarks.

10
00:00:32.254 --> 00:00:34.000
The plan was a starting point, not a ceiling.

11
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:34.558
docs/roadmap.

12
00:00:34.558 --> 00:00:35.630
md tracks what's current.

13
00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:38.634
Its Shipped table is long — nearly everything in this series is on it.

14
00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:47.000
What's left sits in two tiers: Tier 1 is release readiness, needing secrets rather than code; Tier 2 is real feature work, like better avatar lip-sync and semantic search across all chat history.

15
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.690
And the single most-referenced open item across the whole doc set, in both roadmap.

16
00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:50.176
md and release.

17
00:00:50.176 --> 00:00:50.824
md, is code-signing.

18
00:00:50.824 --> 00:00:54.778
The installer isn't signed with a real certificate yet — that's exactly why E02 had you click through Windows SmartScreen.

19
00:00:54.778 --> 00:01:01.000
It's not a technical gap; the pipeline is wired and waiting, it's purely a procurement step: buy a certificate, set two repository secrets, and every future install stops tripping SmartScreen.

20
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.821
Two companion documents are worth reading on your own time if you want to understand why the app's shape is what it is: lm-studio-plan.

21
00:01:05.821 --> 00:01:08.893
md, on how local model hosting actually works under the hood, and claude-desktop-plan.

22
00:01:08.893 --> 00:01:12.000
md, on the instruction-layering design from E11 and E27 and where it was borrowed from.

23
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:21.603
If you're going to contribute, the pattern across every developer episode in this module holds: read the existing tests before writing code, keep side effects thin and pure logic tested — E71 — and land big features as a sequence of small, independently useful commits — E69.

24
00:01:21.603 --> 00:01:23.000
That's genuinely how this codebase grew.

25
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:23.385
The end.

26
00:01:23.385 --> 00:01:31.702
Nineteen modules, seventy-four episodes, from installing on Windows through booting Linux in a browser tab to reading the code itself — thank you for watching the whole way.

27
00:01:31.702 --> 00:01:33.000
Go build something with it.

